.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt XTC_APP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_app_create ,
.Nm xtc_app_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_app_start ,
.Nm xtc_app_run ,
.Nm xtc_app_stop ,
.Nm xtc_app_shutdown ,
.Nm xtc_app_drain_on_signal ,
.Nm xtc_app_is_shutdown_msg ,
.Nm xtc_app_loop ,
.Nm xtc_app_exec ,
.Nm xtc_app_registry
.Nd OTP-style application container
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_app.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_create "const xtc_app_opts_t *opts" "xtc_app_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_app_destroy "xtc_app_t *app"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_start "xtc_app_t *app" "const xtc_child_spec_t *children" "int n_children"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_run "xtc_app_t *app"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_stop "xtc_app_t *app"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_shutdown "xtc_app_t *app" "int64_t drain_ns" "int64_t force_ns" "xtc_app_drain_report_t *report"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_drain_on_signal "xtc_app_t *app" "int64_t drain_ns" "int64_t force_ns" "xtc_app_drain_report_t *report"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_app_is_shutdown_msg "const void *msg" "size_t len"
.Ft "xtc_loop_t *"
.Fn xtc_app_loop "const xtc_app_t *app"
.Ft "xtc_exec_t *"
.Fn xtc_app_exec "const xtc_app_t *app"
.Ft "xtc_reg_t *"
.Fn xtc_app_registry "const xtc_app_t *app"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_app
is the L4 application container -- a root supervisor plus a
process registry plus lifecycle plumbing.  Models OTP's
.Sq application
concept.
.Pp
An app owns:
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
A loop
.Pq created if not supplied .
.It
A root supervisor with the configured children.
.It
A process registry where children can find each other by name
.Pq see Xr xtc_reg 3 .
.El
.Pp
Typical usage:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_app_opts_t opts = XTC_APP_OPTS_DEFAULT;
opts.name = "my_service";
opts.sup.strategy = XTC_SUP_ONE_FOR_ONE;

xtc_app_t *app;
xtc_app_create(&opts, &app);

xtc_child_spec_t kids[] = {
    { .name = "listener",  .fn = listener_proc, .arg = NULL,
      .policy = XTC_RESTART_PERMANENT },
    { .name = "metrics",   .fn = metrics_proc,  .arg = NULL,
      .policy = XTC_RESTART_TRANSIENT },
};
xtc_app_start(app, kids, 2);
xtc_app_run(app);              /* blocks until app stops */
xtc_app_destroy(app);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xtc_app_loop
returns the application's event loop and
.Fn xtc_app_registry
its process registry.
.Fn xtc_app_exec
returns the underlying
.Xr xtc_exec 3
executor the application runs its loop on.
.Pp
Setting
.Fa opts.n_loops
above 1 makes the app own an N-loop executor; the root supervisor runs
on loop 0 and children are placed by
.Fa xtc_child_spec_t.loop .
.Ss Stopping
.Fn xtc_app_stop
is NOT a drain.  It asks the root supervisor to stop and returns at
once; the supervisor then
.Fn xtc_exit_pid Ns s
every child with reason 1 and does not wait for their cleanup.  A child
masked with
.Fn xtc_mask_enter
never observes that kill, and
.Fn xtc_app_run
then never returns.
.Ss Graceful drain
.Fn xtc_app_shutdown
is the bounded, reported drain:
.Bl -enum -offset indent
.It
It sends the message
.Dv XTC_APP_SHUTDOWN_MSG
.Pq Qq $xtc_shutdown ,
.Li sizeof XTC_APP_SHUTDOWN_MSG
bytes with the NUL, to every static child (the children given to
.Fn xtc_app_start ) .
A child recognizes it with
.Fn xtc_app_is_shutdown_msg
and finishes by returning from its body.
.It
It waits up to
.Fa drain_ns
for every child to return or exit.  A PERMANENT child that returns
during the drain is parked, not restarted.
.It
It stops the supervisor (no more restarts) and force-cancels each
remaining child with
.Fn xtc_exit_pid pid XTC_APP_SHUTDOWN_REASON .
.It
It waits up to
.Fa force_ns
for every child to leave the proc table
.Po
.Fn xtc_proc_info
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
.Pc .
That is the cleanup-complete signal: the at-exit hooks and scope
finalizers have returned.  Neither
.Dq not alive
nor
.Dv XTC_KILL_DELIVERED
means that.
.It
It stops the app's loop or executor, so
.Fn xtc_app_run
returns, and fills
.Fa *report .
.El
.Pp
It always returns within about
.Fa drain_ns No + Fa force_ns
plus a few milliseconds, even when a child is masked forever: that
child is reported as a survivor instead of being waited for.  A
survivor on an owned loop is freed with the app; on a borrowed loop
.Pq Fa opts.loop
it stays parked there.
.Pp
The report is size-versioned: set
.Fa report->size
to
.Li sizeof(xtc_app_drain_report_t)
first
.Pq Dv XTC_APP_DRAIN_REPORT_INIT does that .
The library writes only the first
.Fa size
bytes, so fields added in a later minor release do not break an
older caller.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct xtc_app_drain_report {
    size_t    size;           /* IN: sizeof the struct */
    int       n_children;     /* static children */
    int       n_drained;      /* finished within drain_ns */
    int       n_forced;       /* force-cancelled, then left the table */
    int       n_survivors;    /* still in the proc table */
    uint64_t  forced_mask;    /* bit i = child i forced (i < 64) */
    uint64_t  survivor_mask;  /* bit i = child i survived (i < 64) */
    int64_t   elapsed_ns;
} xtc_app_drain_report_t;
.Ed
.Pp
Call
.Fn xtc_app_shutdown
from a plain thread or from a proc that is not one of the app's
supervised children.  Children added later with
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child
are not drained or reported; the supervisor stop in step 3 kills them.
.Ss Signals
The library installs no signal handler by default.
.Fn xtc_app_drain_on_signal
is the opt-in: it installs a SIGTERM and SIGINT handler that only
writes one byte to a non-blocking self-pipe, and spawns a watcher proc
on the app's loop that waits on the pipe with
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
and runs
.Fn xtc_app_shutdown app drain_ns force_ns report
there.  No work runs in the handler.
.Fa report
(may be NULL) must stay valid until
.Fn xtc_app_run
returns.  Call it after
.Fn xtc_app_start
and before
.Fn xtc_app_run ,
on an app that owns its loop.
.Fn xtc_app_destroy
restores the previous dispositions.  One app per process may be armed
at a time.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_app_shutdown
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
when every child left the proc table,
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
when at least one survived, and
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL or unstarted app, a negative deadline, a
.Fa report
whose
.Fa size
is too small, a second call, or a call from a supervised child.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_app_drain_on_signal
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
(bad arguments, a borrowed loop, or another app already armed),
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
if the pipe could not be created, or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
on Windows.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_app_is_shutdown_msg
returns 1 for the drain request and 0 otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa docs/_includes/snippets/12_graceful_drain.c
starts workers, sends itself SIGTERM, drains and exits 0.
